<!-- Chapter 1 answers.

    -->

<section xml:id="ch1-answers" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Answers to Ch. 1 Exercises</title>

    <p>
      Answers to the first four exercises of this chapter. Click
      <term>Solution</term> under any part to reveal its answer.
    </p>
    <p>
      These are answers, not worked solutions. Enough is shown that you can
      tell whether your own work landed in the right place, but the
      intermediate steps are yours to supply. Where a question asks for a
      tree or for backward induction shown graphically, the picture is the
      answer, so the picture is given.
    </p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <paragraphs xml:id="ans-hw-commissioners">
    <title>1. The Pay Increase</title>

    <example xml:id="ans-commissioners-payoffs">
      <title>(a) The four unknown terminal payoffs</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine the payoffs at the four terminal nodes marked
          <m>(?,?,?)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          From left to right: <m>(v-c,\,v-c,\,v-c)</m>,
          <m>(v-c,\,v-c,\,v)</m>, <m>(v-c,\,v,\,v-c)</m>, and
          <m>(-c,\,0,\,0)</m>. Only the last of these is a node where the
          measure fails: <m>A</m> voted yes alone, so <m>A</m> pays
          <m>c</m> and nobody collects <m>v</m>.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ans-commissioners-payoffs">
          <caption>
            The pay increase game with all payoffs filled in. Payoffs are
            listed in the order <m>(A, B, C)</m>.
          </caption>
          <image width="100%">
            <shortdescription>
              The pay increase game tree with all eight terminal payoffs
              filled in.
            </shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
              \resizebox{15cm}{!}{%
              \begin{tikzpicture}[
                font=\scriptsize,
                nd/.style={circle,fill=black,inner sep=1.3pt}
              ]
              \useasboundingbox (-11.3,-1.1) rectangle (11.3,5.4);
              \node[nd] (A) at (0,4.5) {};
              \node[above=3pt of A] {$A$};
              \node[nd] (B1) at (-5.6,3) {};
              \node[above left=1pt and 2pt of B1] {$B$};
              \node[nd] (B2) at (5.6,3) {};
              \node[above right=1pt and 2pt of B2] {$B$};
              \node[nd] (C1) at (-8.4,1.5) {};
              \node[left=2pt of C1] {$C$};
              \node[nd] (C2) at (-2.8,1.5) {};
              \node[left=2pt of C2] {$C$};
              \node[nd] (C3) at (2.8,1.5) {};
              \node[left=2pt of C3] {$C$};
              \node[nd] (C4) at (8.4,1.5) {};
              \node[left=2pt of C4] {$C$};
              \node[nd] (t1) at (-9.8,0) {};
              \node[nd] (t2) at (-7,0) {};
              \node[nd] (t3) at (-4.2,0) {};
              \node[nd] (t4) at (-1.4,0) {};
              \node[nd] (t5) at (1.4,0) {};
              \node[nd] (t6) at (4.2,0) {};
              \node[nd] (t7) at (7,0) {};
              \node[nd] (t8) at (9.8,0) {};
              \node[below=3pt of t1] {$(v-c,\,v-c,\,v-c)$};
              \node[below=3pt of t2] {$(v-c,\,v-c,\,v)$};
              \node[below=3pt of t3] {$(v-c,\,v,\,v-c)$};
              \node[below=3pt of t4] {$(-c,\,0,\,0)$};
              \node[below=3pt of t5] {$(v,\,v-c,\,v-c)$};
              \node[below=3pt of t6] {$(0,\,-c,\,0)$};
              \node[below=3pt of t7] {$(0,\,0,\,-c)$};
              \node[below=3pt of t8] {$(0,\,0,\,0)$};
              \draw (A) -- node[above left] {$y$} (B1);
              \draw (A) -- node[above right] {$n$} (B2);
              \draw (B1) -- node[above left] {$y$} (C1);
              \draw (B1) -- node[above right] {$n$} (C2);
              \draw (B2) -- node[above left] {$y$} (C3);
              \draw (B2) -- node[above right] {$n$} (C4);
              \draw (C1) -- node[above left] {$y$} (t1);
              \draw (C1) -- node[above right] {$n$} (t2);
              \draw (C2) -- node[above left] {$y$} (t3);
              \draw (C2) -- node[above right] {$n$} (t4);
              \draw (C3) -- node[above left] {$y$} (t5);
              \draw (C3) -- node[above right] {$n$} (t6);
              \draw (C4) -- node[above left] {$y$} (t7);
              \draw (C4) -- node[above right] {$n$} (t8);
              \end{tikzpicture}}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ans-commissioners-counting">
      <title>(b) Sizes of the strategy sets</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          How many strategies does each commissioner have?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>|S_A| = 2</m>, <m>|S_B| = 2^2 = 4</m>,
          <m>|S_C| = 2^4 = 16</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ans-commissioners-strategy-words">
      <title>(c) One of C's strategies, in words</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write out one of <m>C</m>'s strategies and describe what it means.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Any of the sixteen is acceptable. Listing <m>C</m>'s move at each
          of his four nodes, from left to right, gives a strategy. For
          example, the strategy <m>n\,n\,n\,y</m> corresponds to the
          following in words:
          <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>A</m> chooses <m>y</m> and <m>B</m> chooses <m>y</m>,
              then <m>C</m> chooses <m>n</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>A</m> chooses <m>y</m> and <m>B</m> chooses <m>n</m>,
              then <m>C</m> chooses <m>n</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>A</m> chooses <m>n</m> and <m>B</m> chooses <m>y</m>,
              then <m>C</m> chooses <m>n</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>A</m> chooses <m>n</m> and <m>B</m> chooses <m>n</m>,
              then <m>C</m> chooses <m>y</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ans-commissioners-bi">
      <title>(d) Backward induction</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Analyze the game by backward induction and indicate the path of
          play.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The diagram of the backward induction is below, with highlighted
          branches that are chosen at each step of the process. The outcome
          is the path <m>A: n</m>, <m>B: y</m>, <m>C: y</m>. The payoffs
          are <m>(v,\,v-c,\,v-c)</m>.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ans-commissioners-bi">
          <caption>
            Backward induction on the pay increase game. Thick edges are
            the selected moves; the boxed payoff is the predicted outcome.
          </caption>
          <image width="100%">
            <shortdescription>
              The pay increase game tree with the backward induction moves
              drawn as thick edges.
            </shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
              \resizebox{15cm}{!}{%
              \begin{tikzpicture}[
                font=\scriptsize,
                nd/.style={circle,fill=black,inner sep=1.3pt},
                pick/.style={line width=1.6pt}
              ]
              \useasboundingbox (-11.3,-1.1) rectangle (11.3,5.4);
              \node[nd] (A) at (0,4.5) {};
              \node[above=3pt of A] {$A$};
              \node[nd] (B1) at (-5.6,3) {};
              \node[above left=1pt and 2pt of B1] {$B$};
              \node[nd] (B2) at (5.6,3) {};
              \node[above right=1pt and 2pt of B2] {$B$};
              \node[nd] (C1) at (-8.4,1.5) {};
              \node[left=2pt of C1] {$C$};
              \node[nd] (C2) at (-2.8,1.5) {};
              \node[left=2pt of C2] {$C$};
              \node[nd] (C3) at (2.8,1.5) {};
              \node[left=2pt of C3] {$C$};
              \node[nd] (C4) at (8.4,1.5) {};
              \node[left=2pt of C4] {$C$};
              \node[nd] (t1) at (-9.8,0) {};
              \node[nd] (t2) at (-7,0) {};
              \node[nd] (t3) at (-4.2,0) {};
              \node[nd] (t4) at (-1.4,0) {};
              \node[nd] (t5) at (1.4,0) {};
              \node[nd] (t6) at (4.2,0) {};
              \node[nd] (t7) at (7,0) {};
              \node[nd] (t8) at (9.8,0) {};
              \node[below=3pt of t1] {$(v-c,\,v-c,\,v-c)$};
              \node[below=3pt of t2] {$(v-c,\,v-c,\,v)$};
              \node[below=3pt of t3] {$(v-c,\,v,\,v-c)$};
              \node[below=3pt of t4] {$(-c,\,0,\,0)$};
              \node[below=3pt of t5,draw,rounded corners,inner sep=3pt]
                {$(v,\,v-c,\,v-c)$};
              \node[below=3pt of t6] {$(0,\,-c,\,0)$};
              \node[below=3pt of t7] {$(0,\,0,\,-c)$};
              \node[below=3pt of t8] {$(0,\,0,\,0)$};
              \draw (A) -- node[above left] {$y$} (B1);
              \draw[pick] (A) -- node[above right] {$n$} (B2);
              \draw (B1) -- node[above left] {$y$} (C1);
              \draw[pick] (B1) -- node[above right] {$n$} (C2);
              \draw[pick] (B2) -- node[above left] {$y$} (C3);
              \draw (B2) -- node[above right] {$n$} (C4);
              \draw (C1) -- node[above left] {$y$} (t1);
              \draw[pick] (C1) -- node[above right] {$n$} (t2);
              \draw[pick] (C2) -- node[above left] {$y$} (t3);
              \draw (C2) -- node[above right] {$n$} (t4);
              \draw[pick] (C3) -- node[above left] {$y$} (t5);
              \draw (C3) -- node[above right] {$n$} (t6);
              \draw (C4) -- node[above left] {$y$} (t7);
              \draw[pick] (C4) -- node[above right] {$n$} (t8);
              \end{tikzpicture}}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ans-commissioners-c-strategy">
      <title>(e) C's rational strategy</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          According to your backward induction analysis, what is <m>C</m>'s
          rational strategy?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The rational strategy is the rational decision <m>C</m> would
          make at each node he is at. We start with the move at the
          leftmost node, which gives the strategy
          <m>s_C = (n,\,y,\,y,\,n)</m>. In words, <m>C</m> votes yes
          exactly when his vote is the deciding one.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </paragraphs>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <paragraphs xml:id="ans-hw-four-player-entry">
    <title>2. Four Players and an Entry Fee</title>

    <example xml:id="ans-four-player-tree">
      <title>(a) The extensive form</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch the extensive form, reporting each player's payoff as a
          net amount.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The diagram of the tree is given below.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ans-four-player-tree">
          <caption>
            The entry fee game in extensive form. Payoffs are net amounts,
            listed in the order <m>(P_1, P_2, P_3, P_4)</m>.
          </caption>
          <image width="100%">
            <shortdescription>
              A chain of four decision nodes, each with an end branch and
              a continue branch.
            </shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
              \resizebox{15cm}{!}{%
              \begin{tikzpicture}[
                font=\scriptsize,
                nd/.style={circle,fill=black,inner sep=1.3pt}
              ]
              \useasboundingbox (-2.6,-3.1) rectangle (14.6,1.2);
              \node[nd] (p1) at (0,0) {};
              \node[above=4pt of p1] {$P_1$};
              \node[nd] (p2) at (3.6,0) {};
              \node[above=4pt of p2] {$P_2$};
              \node[nd] (p3) at (7.2,0) {};
              \node[above=4pt of p3] {$P_3$};
              \node[nd] (p4) at (10.8,0) {};
              \node[above=4pt of p4] {$P_4$};
              \node[nd] (end) at (14.4,0) {};
              \node[nd] (e1) at (0,-2) {};
              \node[nd] (e2) at (3.6,-2) {};
              \node[nd] (e3) at (7.2,-2) {};
              \node[nd] (e4) at (10.8,-2) {};
              \node[below=3pt of e1] {$(-2,\,10,\,10,\,10)$};
              \node[below=3pt of e2] {$(10,\,-2,\,10,\,10)$};
              \node[below=3pt of e3] {$(10,\,10,\,-2,\,10)$};
              \node[below=3pt of e4] {$(10,\,10,\,10,\,-2)$};
              \node[below=3pt of end] {$(-1,\,-1,\,-1,\,-1)$};
              \draw (p1) -- node[left] {$E$} (e1);
              \draw (p2) -- node[left] {$E$} (e2);
              \draw (p3) -- node[left] {$E$} (e3);
              \draw (p4) -- node[left] {$E$} (e4);
              \draw (p1) -- node[above] {$C$} (p2);
              \draw (p2) -- node[above] {$C$} (p3);
              \draw (p3) -- node[above] {$C$} (p4);
              \draw (p4) -- node[above] {$C$} (end);
              \end{tikzpicture}}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ans-four-player-bi">
      <title>(b) The backward induction solution</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Determine the backward induction solution.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Every player continues. The predicted path is <m>C, C, C, C</m>
          and the payoff is <m>(-1,\,-1,\,-1,\,-1)</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </paragraphs>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <paragraphs xml:id="ans-hw-three-player-tree">
    <title>3. A Three Player Game</title>

    <example xml:id="ans-three-player-counting">
      <title>(a) Sizes of the strategy sets</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each player <m>i</m>, determine <m>|S_i|</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>|S_1| = 3 \cdot 3 = 9</m>, <m>|S_2| = 2 \cdot 3 = 6</m>,
          <m>|S_3| = 3 \cdot 4 = 12</m>. Player 1 moves at the root (three
          moves) and at the node reached by <m>z, c</m> (three moves);
          player 2 moves at two nodes, with two and three moves; player 3
          moves at two nodes, with three and four moves. Multiply the
          number of moves across a player's own nodes.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ans-three-player-describe">
      <title>(b) What the strategy <m>x\,b</m> means</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Describe in words what is meant by <m>s_3 = x\,b</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          If player 1 plays <m>y</m> and player 2 responds with <m>a</m>,
          then player 3 plays <m>x</m>. If player 1 plays <m>z</m>, then
          player 3 plays <m>b</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ans-three-player-bi">
      <title>(c) Backward induction</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Analyze the game by backward induction and indicate the path of
          play.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The path of play is <m>x</m> then <m>y</m>, ending with a payoff
          <m>(0,\,6,\,2)</m>. The highlighted moves that are chosen at each
          step in backward induction are shown below.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ans-three-player-bi">
          <caption>
            Backward induction on the three player game. Thick edges are
            the selected moves; the boxed payoff is the predicted outcome.
          </caption>
          <image width="100%">
            <shortdescription>
              The three player game tree with the backward induction moves
              drawn as thick edges.
            </shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
              \resizebox{15cm}{!}{%
              \begin{tikzpicture}[
                font=\scriptsize,
                nd/.style={circle,fill=black,inner sep=1.3pt},
                pick/.style={line width=1.6pt}
              ]
              \useasboundingbox (-11.3,-1.1) rectangle (11.3,6.6);
              \node[nd] (r) at (-2,6) {};
              \node[above=3pt of r] {$1$};
              \node[nd] (h1) at (-9.35,4) {};
              \node[above left=1pt and 2pt of h1] {$2$};
              \node[nd] (h2) at (-2.27,4) {};
              \node[above right=1pt and 2pt of h2] {$2$};
              \node[nd] (h4) at (5.53,4) {};
              \node[above right=1pt and 2pt of h4] {$3$};
              \node[nd] (a1) at (-10.2,2) {};
              \node[nd] (a2) at (-8.5,2) {};
              \node[below=3pt of a1] {$(30,5,0)$};
              \node[below=3pt of a2,draw,rounded corners,inner sep=3pt]
                {$(0,6,2)$};
              \node[nd] (h3) at (-5.1,2) {};
              \node[left=2pt of h3] {$3$};
              \node[nd] (b2) at (-1.7,2) {};
              \node[nd] (b3) at (0,2) {};
              \node[below=3pt of b2] {$(20,3,3)$};
              \node[below=3pt of b3] {$(-1,20,1)$};
              \node[nd] (c1) at (-6.8,0) {};
              \node[nd] (c2) at (-5.1,0) {};
              \node[nd] (c3) at (-3.4,0) {};
              \node[below=3pt of c1] {$(10,6,5)$};
              \node[below=3pt of c2] {$(7,-5,15)$};
              \node[below=3pt of c3] {$(4,15,2)$};
              \node[nd] (d1) at (1.7,2) {};
              \node[nd] (d2) at (3.4,2) {};
              \node[nd] (h5) at (6.8,2) {};
              \node[right=2pt of h5] {$1$};
              \node[nd] (d4) at (10.2,2) {};
              \node[below=3pt of d1] {$(-2,8,10)$};
              \node[below=3pt of d2] {$(10,12,8)$};
              \node[below=3pt of d4] {$(12,5,7)$};
              \node[nd] (e1) at (5.1,0) {};
              \node[nd] (e2) at (6.8,0) {};
              \node[nd] (e3) at (8.5,0) {};
              \node[below=3pt of e1] {$(8,-2,3)$};
              \node[below=3pt of e2] {$(5,10,5)$};
              \node[below=3pt of e3] {$(6,12,8)$};
              \draw[pick] (r) -- node[above left] {$x$} (h1);
              \draw (r) -- node[left] {$y$} (h2);
              \draw (r) -- node[above right] {$z$} (h4);
              \draw (h1) -- node[above left] {$x$} (a1);
              \draw[pick] (h1) -- node[above right] {$y$} (a2);
              \draw (h2) -- node[above left] {$a$} (h3);
              \draw (h2) -- node[left] {$b$} (b2);
              \draw[pick] (h2) -- node[above right] {$c$} (b3);
              \draw (h3) -- node[above left] {$x$} (c1);
              \draw[pick] (h3) -- node[left] {$y$} (c2);
              \draw (h3) -- node[above right] {$z$} (c3);
              \draw[pick] (h4) -- node[above left] {$a$} (d1);
              \draw (h4) -- node[left] {$b$} (d2);
              \draw (h4) -- node[right] {$c$} (h5);
              \draw (h4) -- node[above right] {$d$} (d4);
              \draw[pick] (h5) -- node[above left] {$a$} (e1);
              \draw (h5) -- node[left] {$b$} (e2);
              \draw (h5) -- node[above right] {$c$} (e3);
              \end{tikzpicture}}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ans-three-player-strategies">
      <title>(d) Each player's strategy</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          What strategy will each player use?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Taking each player's nodes left to right, <m>s_1 = x\,a</m>,
          <m>s_2 = y\,c</m>, and <m>s_3 = y\,a</m>. Each of these names a
          move at both of that player's nodes, including the ones off the
          predicted path. Reporting only <m>x</m> for player 1, or only
          <m>y</m> for player 2, gives a move rather than a strategy.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ans-three-player-preferred">
      <title>(e) An outcome all three prefer</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Identify an outcome all three players strictly prefer to the
          backward induction outcome.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The predicted outcome leads to a payoff <m>(0,\,6,\,2)</m>, so we
          need an outcome beating <m>0</m>, <m>6</m>, and <m>2</m> in all
          three coordinates at once. The following three all work:
          <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              The terminal node <m>(10,\,12,\,8)</m>, reached by <m>1</m>
              playing <m>z</m> and <m>3</m> playing <m>b</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The terminal node with a payoff of <m>(5,\,10,\,5)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              The terminal node with a payoff of <m>(6,\,12,\,8)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </paragraphs>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <paragraphs xml:id="ans-hw-backward-induction-fails">
    <title>4. When Backward Induction Fails</title>

    <example xml:id="ans-bi-fails-a">
      <title>(a) Where Game A breaks down</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          At which node does the procedure break down in Game A, and why
          does that leave player 1 unable to decide?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          It breaks down at player 2's node. Both <m>c</m> and <m>d</m> pay
          player 2 the same amount, <m>1</m>, so there is no unique best
          move there and no single payoff pair to fold back up to the root.
        </p>
        <p>
          Player 1 is then stuck. He does not know whether choosing
          <m>a</m> or <m>b</m> will lead to a better payoff, because he
          does not know what player 2 will choose at the right node.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ans-bi-fails-b">
      <title>(b) The same breakdown in Game B</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that backward induction breaks down at player 2's node in
          Game B for the same reason.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Player 2 gets <m>2</m> from <m>c</m> and <m>2</m> from <m>d</m>,
          so the payoffs are equal and backward induction cannot predict
          what happens at this node.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ans-bi-fails-b-obvious">
      <title>(c) Player 1's rational choice in Game B</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Identify player 1's rational choice and explain why he can make
          it without knowing what player 2 would do.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Player 1 should play <m>a</m>. It pays him <m>5</m>, while the
          only payoffs he can reach after <m>b</m> are <m>4</m> and
          <m>1</m>, and <m>5</m> beats both. He therefore does not need to
          know, or guess, how player 2 breaks her tie: whichever move she
          would make, <m>a</m> leaves him better off.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </paragraphs>
</section>
